1
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:14,779
You have entered university
to study Computer Science.

2
00:00:14,829 --> 00:00:17,043
You may be excited.

3
00:00:17,093 --> 00:00:23,440
You may believe that the next three or
four years will teach you everything you

4
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,593
need to become a software engineer.

5
00:00:26,643 --> 00:00:31,857
But there is something you need
to understand from day one.

6
00:00:31,907 --> 00:00:37,121
Computer Science is one of the
fastest-changing fields in the world.

7
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:44,207
Your university will give you something
extremely valuable: foundations.

8
00:00:44,257 --> 00:00:46,471
You will learn programming.

9
00:00:46,521 --> 00:00:50,439
You will learn algorithms, databases,
operating systems, networking, software

10
00:00:50,479 --> 00:00:53,450
engineering, mathematics
and computer science theory.

11
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:55,714
You will complete assignments.

12
00:00:55,764 --> 00:00:57,979
You will sit examinations.

13
00:00:58,029 --> 00:01:01,529
You will eventually
complete a major project.

14
00:01:01,579 --> 00:01:05,507
But your degree is not the finish line.

15
00:01:05,557 --> 00:01:08,200
It is the starting point.

16
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:14,779
Technology does not wait for
your university syllabus.

17
00:01:14,829 --> 00:01:16,829
Programming languages evolve.

18
00:01:16,879 --> 00:01:18,879
Frameworks disappear.

19
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,571
New frameworks replace old ones.

20
00:01:21,621 --> 00:01:23,621
Development methodologies change.

21
00:01:23,671 --> 00:01:27,171
Cloud computing changes
how applications are built.

22
00:01:27,221 --> 00:01:31,579
Artificial intelligence is changing
how software itself is created.

23
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,564
Java is still important.

24
00:01:36,614 --> 00:01:41,829
Learning one programming language teaches
you concepts that transfer to others.

25
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:47,803
But do not make the mistake of believing
that mastering one language means you have

26
00:01:47,843 --> 00:01:49,664
mastered programming.

27
00:01:49,714 --> 00:01:51,929
Learn programming concepts first.

28
00:01:51,979 --> 00:01:54,621
Then learn languages as tools.

29
00:01:57,350 --> 00:01:59,993
Web development is another example.

30
00:02:00,043 --> 00:02:04,522
Do not spend your entire university
journey learning yesterday's development

31
00:02:04,562 --> 00:02:05,257
stack.

32
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:07,307
Understand the fundamentals.

33
00:02:07,357 --> 00:02:09,357
But keep asking:

34
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:12,050
What is being used today?

35
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:14,743
What is becoming important tomorrow?

36
00:02:14,793 --> 00:02:16,793
And most importantly:

37
00:02:16,843 --> 00:02:19,914
Can I build something with it?

38
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:25,850
Artificial Intelligence is
not one single technology.

39
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:28,971
It is a family of technologies.

40
00:02:29,021 --> 00:02:31,021
Machine learning.

41
00:02:31,071 --> 00:02:33,071
Deep learning.

42
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:35,121
Data science.

43
00:02:35,171 --> 00:02:37,171
Computer vision.

44
00:02:37,221 --> 00:02:39,221
Natural language processing.

45
00:02:39,271 --> 00:02:41,271
Generative AI.

46
00:02:41,321 --> 00:02:46,536
And increasingly, AI agents capable
of using tools and performing tasks.

47
00:02:48,850 --> 00:02:53,207
And here is something many
students discover too late.

48
00:02:53,257 --> 00:02:55,257
Mathematics matters.

49
00:02:55,307 --> 00:02:57,307
Statistics matters.

50
00:02:57,357 --> 00:02:59,357
Probability matters.

51
00:02:59,407 --> 00:03:01,407
Linear algebra matters.

52
00:03:01,457 --> 00:03:06,842
You may wonder why you need them
when modern libraries can perform the

53
00:03:06,882 --> 00:03:08,814
calculations for you.

54
00:03:08,864 --> 00:03:11,079
The answer is simple.

55
00:03:11,129 --> 00:03:13,129
Tools can calculate.

56
00:03:13,179 --> 00:03:17,536
But you need to understand
what the calculation means.

57
00:03:17,586 --> 00:03:22,955
If you want to move beyond simply using
AI tools and start understanding AI, data

58
00:03:22,995 --> 00:03:27,631
science and machine learning, mathematics
and statistics become powerful

59
00:03:27,671 --> 00:03:28,800
foundations.

60
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,564
Do not misunderstand this.

61
00:03:33,614 --> 00:03:35,614
Theory is important.

62
00:03:35,664 --> 00:03:39,593
But theory without application
can leave you unprepared.

63
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:42,714
Your goal should not simply be:

64
00:03:42,764 --> 00:03:44,979
'I passed the examination.'

65
00:03:45,029 --> 00:03:47,243
Your goal should be:

66
00:03:47,293 --> 00:03:54,221
'I understand the concept — and I
can use it to solve a real problem.'

67
00:03:56,850 --> 00:04:00,350
The technology industry
does not simply ask:

68
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:03,043
'What subjects did you study?'

69
00:04:03,093 --> 00:04:05,093
It increasingly asks:

70
00:04:05,143 --> 00:04:07,357
'What can you build?'

71
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:10,050
'What problems can you solve?'

72
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,314
'Can you learn quickly?'

73
00:04:12,364 --> 00:04:15,436
'Can you work with modern tools?'

74
00:04:15,486 --> 00:04:18,557
'Can you collaborate with other people?'

75
00:04:18,607 --> 00:04:20,607
And increasingly:

76
00:04:20,657 --> 00:04:25,871
'Can you use AI effectively without
losing your ability to think?'

77
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:30,993
Artificial intelligence
creates another challenge.

78
00:04:31,043 --> 00:04:34,114
You can now generate an essay.

79
00:04:34,164 --> 00:04:36,164
Generate code.

80
00:04:36,214 --> 00:04:38,214
Generate diagrams.

81
00:04:38,264 --> 00:04:40,264
Generate explanations.

82
00:04:40,314 --> 00:04:43,386
Even generate an entire project structure.

83
00:04:43,436 --> 00:04:46,507
But there is a dangerous shortcut:

84
00:04:46,557 --> 00:04:50,057
using AI without understanding
what it produces.

85
00:04:50,107 --> 00:04:54,036
AI should accelerate learning
— not replace learning.

86
00:04:54,086 --> 00:04:59,300
Use AI as a tutor, coding assistant,
reviewer and brainstorming partner.

87
00:04:59,350 --> 00:05:03,707
But make sure you can
explain what you submit.

88
00:05:03,757 --> 00:05:09,246
Otherwise, you may pass an assignment
while failing to develop the skill the

89
00:05:09,286 --> 00:05:11,971
assignment was supposed to teach.

90
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:19,564
Do not wait until your final
year to build your portfolio.

91
00:05:19,614 --> 00:05:21,614
Start early.

92
00:05:21,664 --> 00:05:23,664
Build small projects.

93
00:05:23,714 --> 00:05:25,714
Break them.

94
00:05:25,764 --> 00:05:27,764
Fix them.

95
00:05:27,814 --> 00:05:29,814
Publish them.

96
00:05:29,864 --> 00:05:32,079
Document what you learned.

97
00:05:32,129 --> 00:05:37,075
Your GitHub repositories, projects and
practical experience can become evidence

98
00:05:37,115 --> 00:05:39,057
of what you actually know.

99
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:44,493
The uncomfortable truth is this:

100
00:05:44,543 --> 00:05:50,905
The technology you learn in your first
year may not be the technology you use

101
00:05:50,945 --> 00:05:52,757
when you graduate.

102
00:05:52,807 --> 00:05:55,450
And that is completely normal.

103
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:59,857
Your most important skill is not
memorising today's technology.

104
00:05:59,907 --> 00:06:03,836
It is learning how to learn
tomorrow's technology.

105
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:09,850
And there is another
technology worth watching.

106
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:11,900
Quantum computing.

107
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:17,593
It is still an emerging field, and its
practical impact remains uncertain.

108
00:06:17,643 --> 00:06:23,458
But if quantum computing reaches the level
where it can solve important real-world

109
00:06:23,498 --> 00:06:28,857
problems at scale, it could create
an entirely new chapter in computing.

110
00:06:28,907 --> 00:06:33,783
Perhaps one day, your final-year
project will not simply be another web

111
00:06:33,823 --> 00:06:34,979
application.

112
00:06:35,029 --> 00:06:38,957
Perhaps you will explore a
quantum computing problem.

113
00:06:39,007 --> 00:06:46,134
If the technology matures enough — and if
you are prepared — you could be part of

114
00:06:46,174 --> 00:06:47,650
that journey.

115
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:54,279
You came to university to
study Computer Science.

116
00:06:54,329 --> 00:06:57,829
Do not leave with only a degree.

117
00:06:57,879 --> 00:07:00,950
Leave with the ability to learn.

118
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,643
Leave knowing how to program.

119
00:07:03,693 --> 00:07:06,764
Leave knowing how to solve problems.

120
00:07:06,814 --> 00:07:09,886
Leave understanding mathematics,
data and technology.

121
00:07:09,936 --> 00:07:11,936
Learn the fundamentals.

122
00:07:11,986 --> 00:07:13,986
Use modern tools.

123
00:07:14,036 --> 00:07:16,036
Experiment with AI.

124
00:07:16,086 --> 00:07:18,086
Build real projects.

125
00:07:18,136 --> 00:07:20,350
Do not fear change.

126
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:25,487
Because the biggest technology you
will learn at university may not be a

127
00:07:25,527 --> 00:07:27,329
programming language.

128
00:07:27,379 --> 00:07:30,879
It may be the ability to adapt.

129
00:07:30,929 --> 00:07:32,929
And remember:

130
00:07:32,979 --> 00:07:37,336
The future of computing
has not been written yet.

131
00:07:37,386 --> 00:07:40,457
Perhaps you will help write it.

